# The client-side PKCS#11 module of the experimental Certificate portal.
# See pkcs11.conf(5), and docs/decisions/0011-client-side-pkcs11-module.md.
#
# It presents ONE token, "Portal Certificate", holding the one certificate the
# user chose in the portal's chooser, and forwards signing to the portal. It
# holds no key, no PIN and no card.
#
# NOT CRITICAL. A machine with no Certificate portal, or with the experimental
# gate off, must still load every other module: this one then offers no slot.
#
# OPT IN BY NAME. A credential search through this module can raise a chooser,
# and a program that enumerates PKCS#11 objects at start-up did not ask a user
# for anything. So the shipped file names the consumers that want the portal
# token and nobody else. p11-kit matches the base name of argv[0]; add a name
# to enable-in below, or drop a file of your own into
# ~/.config/pkcs11/modules/xdg-desktop-portal-certificate.module which
# overrides this one.
#
#   xdg-desktop-portal-webauth  the web-authentication portal's backend, which
#                               builds a GTlsCertificate before WebKit will
#                               carry one to its network process
#   WebKitNetworkProcess        WebKitGTK's network process, which owns the TLS
#                               handshake and resolves the URI a second time
#
# For Firefox or Thunderbird, uncomment the line below. NSS reaches PKCS#11 its
# own way and this path has not been tested; expect a second, browser-owned
# certificate dialog on top of the portal's chooser.
#
#     enable-in: firefox, thunderbird
#
# DO NOT ADD disable-in HERE. pkcs11.conf(5) says "Do not specify both
# enable-in and disable-in for the same module", and p11-kit's
# is_module_enabled_unlocked() takes the enable-in branch and returns before it
# ever looks at disable-in: with both set the disable-in is dead weight. An
# allowlist is strictly narrower than a denylist anyway.
#
# MUST NOT BE LOADED BY THE PORTAL ITSELF. xdg-desktop-portal and the
# certificate backend enumerate PKCS#11 modules, and this one calls them back.
# Neither is on the list above, and neither can be added: the module refuses to
# run in a process named after either of them, and the backend skips this
# module by name when it enumerates. Those two fences are in code, because
# pkcs11.conf(5) says enable-in and disable-in are not a security feature.
# OTHER portal backends are ordinary consumers -- xdg-desktop-portal-webauth is
# the first one -- and that is why one of them is on the list.

module: @module@
critical: no
enable-in: xdg-desktop-portal-webauth, WebKitNetworkProcess

# Low, so that a real token is found before this one by anything that walks the
# module list in priority order.
priority: -10
